/*
==================== JOB OBJECT — THEORY (launch Coroutine Builder) ====================

1️⃣ What is a Job?

- Job represents the lifecycle of a coroutine.
- Every coroutine created using `launch` has a Job.
- Even if you don’t store it, a Job is always created internally.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

2️⃣ Why does `launch` return a Job?

- `launch` does NOT return a result.
- Instead of data, Kotlin provides control.
- That control object is Job.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

3️⃣ When does Job come into picture?

- The moment `launch { }` is called.
- Coroutine starts → Job is created → Job becomes ACTIVE.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

4️⃣ What does Job represent?

Job represents:
- Whether the coroutine is running
- Whether it is cancelled
- Whether it has completed
- The ability to stop or wait for the coroutine

---------------------------------------------------------------------

5️⃣ What can we do using Job?

Common Job operations:

- job.cancel()       → Cancels the coroutine
- job.join()         → Suspends until coroutine completes
- job.isActive       → True if coroutine is running
- job.isCancelled    → True if coroutine was cancelled
- job.isCompleted    → True if coroutine finished
- job.start()        → Starts LAZY coroutine only

---------------------------------------------------------------------

6️⃣ Important rule about job.start()

- By default, `launch { }` starts immediately.
- job.start() is useful ONLY when coroutine is created with:

    launch(start = CoroutineStart.LAZY)

- For normal launch, job.start() returns false.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

7️⃣ Typical real-world usage of Job

- Cancel API call when user leaves screen
- Stop search request when user types new text
- Prevent duplicate background tasks
- Control long-running background work

---------------------------------------------------------------------

8️⃣ When Job is usually NOT needed

- Simple UI tasks
- Fire-and-forget operations
- lifecycleScope / viewModelScope coroutines

In these cases, Job exists but is ignored.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

9️⃣ Job vs Deferred

- launch → returns Job → lifecycle control
- async  → returns Deferred<T> → result + control

---------------------------------------------------------------------

🔑 ONE-LINE SUMMARY

Job is a handle to control the lifecycle of a coroutine created by `launch`.

======================================================================================
*/
